WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370
Node.

2
00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:11.264
js applications sometimes call external processes for image processing, document conversion, system utilities, build steps, or operational scripts.

3
00:00:11.264 --> 00:00:22.223
Command injection risk appears when user-controlled input influences the command, arguments, options, environment, file paths, or working directory in an unsafe way.

4
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:28.692
The danger increases when code builds one shell string and asks the operating system to interpret it.

5
00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:32.726
A safer design starts by asking whether a shell is needed at all.

6
00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:42.848
Many tasks can be performed through libraries, platform APIs, or process execution modes that pass a fixed executable and controlled arguments separately.

7
00:00:42.848 --> 00:00:47.186
Avoiding shell interpretation removes a large class of ambiguity.

8
00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:53.579
When external execution is necessary, the set of allowed operations should be narrow and explicit.

9
00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.405
Argument handling matters.

10
00:00:55.405 --> 00:01:03.168
User choices should map to allowlisted operations, file types, or options rather than becoming free-form command text.

11
00:01:03.168 --> 00:01:07.735
File paths should be resolved carefully within intended directories.

12
00:01:07.735 --> 00:01:10.703
Environment variables should be controlled.

13
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:18.770
The process should run with the least privilege practical for the task, and the working directory should not be left to chance.

14
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:23.413
Logging should support investigation without leaking sensitive data.

15
00:01:23.413 --> 00:01:30.262
It is useful to record which approved operation was requested, who requested it, and whether it succeeded.

16
00:01:30.262 --> 00:01:38.482
It is not useful to dump secrets, full command strings containing private values, or untrusted input into logs without thought.

17
00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:41.526
Observability and privacy need to be balanced.

18
00:01:41.526 --> 00:01:55.606
The defensive principle is to avoid string-built commands, separate commands from arguments, constrain what users can request, prefer safer libraries when practical, and treat process execution as a sensitive sink.

19
00:01:55.606 --> 00:02:00.629
The more powerful the command environment, the stricter the design should be.
